WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.588
Welcome to Module 9 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.588 --> 00:00:06.675
govern A I agent identities.

3
00:00:06.675 --> 00:00:10.030
This module teaches the
guide's A I agents section,

4
00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.813
with Chapter 9 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:18.437
A I agents are non-person identities, and
they follow every gate before this module.

6
00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:22.819
In it you'll check a fictional tenant's
agents against the program's rules,

7
00:00:22.819 --> 00:00:26.470
see the same rules run in a cloud
that has no agent identities,

8
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.413
and plan two report-only Conditional
Access policies for agents.

9
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:34.176
Then you'll apply one of them to
a fake, by its approved hash.

10
00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:39.000
The tenant and its agents are fictional,
and the apply changes only a fake.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.870
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

12
00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.370
Why does this module exist?

13
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:54.420
Agents follow every gate before
it, but they add three things.

14
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.245
First, an agent can act.

15
00:00:56.245 --> 00:01:00.532
So an orphaned agent is disabled as
soon as the finding is confirmed.

16
00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:03.782
That's the program's orphan
rule for every identity,

17
00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:06.258
and agents are why it's the stricter one.

18
00:01:06.258 --> 00:01:11.170
Second, Microsoft Learn's Agent I D
documentation says a blueprint holds the

19
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.821
credentials for every agent made from it,

20
00:01:13.821 --> 00:01:17.096
so one secret on a blueprint
is every agent's secret.

21
00:01:17.096 --> 00:01:21.712
And third, Learn's page on administrative
relationships in Agent I D says that by

22
00:01:21.712 --> 00:01:25.070
default the user who creates
an agent becomes its sponsor,

23
00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:29.058
and the guide adds that the creator
becomes the blueprint's owner too.

24
00:01:29.058 --> 00:01:30.944
So creation has to be gated.

25
00:01:30.944 --> 00:01:35.782
In this module you check a fictional
tenant's agents against the program's rules,

26
00:01:35.782 --> 00:01:39.519
see the same rules run where the
cloud has no agent identities,

27
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:43.540
and plan two report-only agent
Conditional Access policies.

28
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.169
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:50.169 --> 00:01:52.852
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

30
00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:56.439
You'll describe a blueprint, an
agent identity and a sponsor,

31
00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:59.440
and why the blueprint is
the credential boundary.

32
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.218
You'll explain decision D N P six as data,

33
00:02:02.218 --> 00:02:05.690
and what the code does in
a cloud without Agent I D.

34
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:09.204
You'll run the A I identity
check and read its findings,

35
00:02:09.204 --> 00:02:11.440
including the top-priority drift.

36
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.081
You'll explain the
ten-business-day sponsor rule,

37
00:02:14.081 --> 00:02:16.128
and what happens to an orphaned agent.

38
00:02:16.128 --> 00:02:20.419
And you'll show that the agent
Conditional Access plan is report-only,

39
00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:22.527
and runs only by its approved hash.

40
00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:27.891
Before you start, finish Module 2, because
the lab reads Chapter 2's ownership run,

41
00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:32.320
and Module 6, on Conditional
Access for workload identities.

42
00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:41.845
Unit 2 starts with what Microsoft Entra
Agent I D is, in Microsoft Learn's words.

43
00:02:41.845 --> 00:02:45.690
A blueprint holds the credentials
and creates agent identities,

44
00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:47.683
and it gets tokens on their behalf.

45
00:02:47.683 --> 00:02:52.007
So a compromise of the blueprint's
credential affects every agent under it.

46
00:02:52.007 --> 00:02:54.820
That's why the blueprint is
the credential boundary.

47
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.158
The agent identities hold the permissions,
and they're what appears in the logs.

48
00:02:59.158 --> 00:03:03.134
The pieces are at different stages,
and Learn's release announcements,

49
00:03:03.134 --> 00:03:05.809
its Conditional Access
for agents page and the

50
00:03:05.809 --> 00:03:08.895
Agent three sixty-five
service description say which.

51
00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:10.893
The core objects, blueprints,

52
00:03:10.893 --> 00:03:14.807
agent identities and sponsors,
are generally available.

53
00:03:14.807 --> 00:03:18.636
Conditional Access for agents
and agent risk are preview,

54
00:03:18.636 --> 00:03:22.783
and the Graph properties for
agent policies exist only in beta.

55
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:26.770
In G C C, a documented subset is listed.

56
00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.932
In G C C High and D O D,
nothing is published yet.

57
00:03:30.932 --> 00:03:34.330
So the program holds its
decision about this as data.

58
00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.621
The program's decision D N P six is
held as data, in a configuration

59
00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:47.505
file with each capability's stage by
cloud, and its status is proposed.

60
00:03:47.505 --> 00:03:49.768
The code reads it and picks a mode.

61
00:03:49.768 --> 00:03:53.435
In the worldwide and G C C clouds
it runs in Agent I D mode:

62
00:03:53.435 --> 00:03:58.568
it reads blueprints and agent identities
and checks them against the rules.

63
00:03:58.568 --> 00:04:03.399
In G C C High and D O D, where
Microsoft Learn's feature availability

64
00:04:03.399 --> 00:04:08.230
page publishes no Agent I D
availability, it runs in fallback mode.

65
00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.647
It sends no agent identity read at all,

66
00:04:10.647 --> 00:04:15.168
and applies the same rules to the
service principals the agents run as.

67
00:04:15.168 --> 00:04:19.168
So the rules don't change by cloud;
only what the code reads does.

68
00:04:19.168 --> 00:04:22.155
Preview features are
reported, never enforced.

69
00:04:22.155 --> 00:04:24.050
That's design guidance.

70
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.225
Here are the program's
rules the code holds.

71
00:04:30.225 --> 00:04:33.550
They're the program's design
guidance, not Microsoft's.

72
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.712
Creation is closed: users
can't create agent blueprints.

73
00:04:37.712 --> 00:04:42.382
Every production agent has a reviewed
handshake before it exists, naming its

74
00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:47.051
sponsor and group, a technical owner who
isn't the sponsor, and its permissions.

75
00:04:47.051 --> 00:04:50.644
Credentials prefer a managed
identity, then federation,

76
00:04:50.644 --> 00:04:54.238
then a certificate, and never
a production client secret.

77
00:04:54.238 --> 00:04:59.332
Least privilege means inheritance is never
allow-all for application roles, because,

78
00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:03.135
as Microsoft Learn's page on
inheritable permissions explains,

79
00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:07.225
any future grant to the blueprint
then becomes a grant to every agent,

80
00:05:07.225 --> 00:05:09.951
and no agent holds a
standing privileged role.

81
00:05:09.951 --> 00:05:13.717
Sponsors are federal employees,
and on a move or leave the new

82
00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:17.937
sponsor confirms within ten business
days, or the agent is orphaned.

83
00:05:17.937 --> 00:05:19.851
Two report-only policies.

84
00:05:19.851 --> 00:05:23.010
And three kinds of drift are top priority.

85
00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:31.497
The checks are twenty-three rules,
N P I A I oh one to twenty-three.

86
00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.518
The first three check that creation
is closed, with a recent reading,

87
00:05:35.518 --> 00:05:38.534
and flag an agent on an
ordinary service principal.

88
00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:43.272
Four to nine check the handshake, made
before the agent existed, and the people:

89
00:05:43.272 --> 00:05:48.010
a sponsor who's an enabled employee, and
a technical owner who isn't the sponsor.

90
00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.185
Ten and eleven are the credentials:

91
00:05:50.185 --> 00:05:53.560
a client secret on a production
blueprint is a finding.

92
00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.748
Twelve to fifteen are privilege.

93
00:05:55.748 --> 00:05:59.118
Sixteen and seventeen are
the program's sponsor clock:

94
00:05:59.118 --> 00:06:03.910
a move or leave unconfirmed after ten
business days makes the agent orphaned.

95
00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.968
The days skip federal holidays,

96
00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:09.610
so a leaver on October ninth
is due October twenty-sixth.

97
00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.016
Eighteen to twenty check the two policies,

98
00:06:12.016 --> 00:06:15.610
and twenty-one to twenty-three
are the top-priority drift.

99
00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.458
The only thing this chapter ever
writes is the two agent policies,

100
00:06:23.458 --> 00:06:25.810
report-only, and that's the kit's design.

101
00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.024
The plan compares the two reviewed
policies with the tenant's,

102
00:06:30.024 --> 00:06:32.998
and says create, in place,
or differs for each.

103
00:06:32.998 --> 00:06:36.145
Where decision D N P six
says agent Conditional

104
00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:39.535
Access isn't published, it
marks them not available.

105
00:06:39.535 --> 00:06:43.298
And it refuses a reviewed file
that isn't report-only Block.

106
00:06:43.298 --> 00:06:45.523
The write path is a separate script.

107
00:06:45.523 --> 00:06:49.537
It refuses to run unless the
plan's hash is the approved one,

108
00:06:49.537 --> 00:06:53.384
for this tenant and cloud, and
no more than seven days old.

109
00:06:53.384 --> 00:06:58.010
Every write creates a report-only
policy with Block as the only grant.

110
00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:02.023
It runs as a person with the
Conditional Access Administrator role.

111
00:07:02.023 --> 00:07:03.960
And it never turns a policy on.

112
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.803
Microsoft Learn's page on Conditional
Access for agents lists the feature as

113
00:07:08.803 --> 00:07:14.230
preview, so that waits until the agent
targets leave preview in the agency's cloud.

114
00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:18.705
Try it.

115
00:07:18.705 --> 00:07:24.380
Lab 1 runs in the non-person identity
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

116
00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:28.659
Its step one makes Chapter 1's register
and Chapter 2's ownership run,

117
00:07:28.659 --> 00:07:31.180
so the chapter's steps are one ahead here.

118
00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.392
The ownership run's last
line is its plan's hash.

119
00:07:34.392 --> 00:07:38.602
Step two sends Microsoft Learn's
Agent I D samples to the fake:

120
00:07:38.602 --> 00:07:43.654
thirteen pass, none fail, and forty-nine
